ng to multiply  that times  the four times this depth  times the four  and we get let's see one half times four  is two  so this these guys cancel out you'll  just have a two and then two times three  is six six times seven is 40  is 42 and it would be in some type of  cubic unit so if these were in i don't  know centimeters it would be centimeters  cubed but they're not making us focus on  the units in this problem let's do  another one  shown as a cube if each side  of the cube or if each side is of equal  length x equals three  what is the total volume of the cube so  each side is equal length x which  happens to equal three so this side is  three  this side over here x is equal to three  every side x is equal to three so it's  actually the same exercise as the  triangular prism it's actually a little  bit easier when you're dealing with the  cube where you really just want to find  the area of this surface right over here  and this is pretty straightforward this  is just a square or you could it would  be the base times the height or since  they're the same it's just three times  three so the volume is going to be the  area of this surface three times three  times the depth  times the depth  and so we go three deep so times  so times three and so we get three times  three times three it's just 27. or you  might recognize this from exponents this  is the same thing as three to the third  power and that's why sometimes if you  have something to the third power  they'll say you cubed it because  literally to find the volume of a cube  you take the length of one side and you  multiply that number by itself three  times one for each dimension one for the  length the width and or i guess the  height the length and the depth  depending on how you want to define them  so it's literally just 3 times 3 times  3.
 

